The client sends one unchanged 9-argument call, rpc_submit_response, whose p_price_rsd it derives locally. For MY_PRICE tasks it derives the price from the same Need read as the revision: the amount, amount x people for PER_PERSON, or the total with every place covered for TOTAL. For OFFERS the worker types the price. The selection command carries no price. The Agreement amount is copied by the server from the stored response version, never from the client.

The premise that the server does not reject a different price is stale. Live DEV already refuses a non-canonical price on all three doors that write a price: submit, stale-resolve and select. Registry row B09 and OFFER_RECEIPT.json line 35 say otherwise. The live submit body (md5 c98e5bee...) equals the PKG-033 receipt md5, so it has not drifted since 2026-09-21. A live probe of the shared rule raised 22023 FIXED_PRICE_MISMATCH for one dinar more.

A reject-only PKG-049 on MY_PRICE is therefore already live and changes nothing for any client. The remaining real questions are policy questions:
- the OFFERS price, which is worker-declared;
- the free bilateral price patch in rpc_propose_agreement_change_v2, which has no link to the task price;
- whether the owner wants "derive" instead of "reject".

Compatibility matters because there is no version gate and no app-version header, so the server cannot tell old builds from new ones. Safe: status quo, reusing the existing error codes and SQLSTATEs, and additive receipt keys. Unsafe:
- a new refusal code, which installed builds map to the generic unconfirmed outcome and then a replay loop;
- a rewrite of covered_slots;
- a silent price rewrite, which the composer's confirmation screen would misstate;
- any move or rename of the directly-read columns.

I also found a latent client dead-end. On the new-offer submit path the price refusal codes are not in the conclusive-refusal allowlist, so the composer locks into "Proveri ishod / Ponovi istu Prijavu" with no way to author a new offer. It is source-derived only, not native-proven, and unreachable while the client sends the canonical price.
